# Openfire did not remove a deleted user's multi-user-chat affiliations, so a
# user registering the same username inherited the old user's room privileges
# (privilege inheritance on identity reuse, CWE-269). Rooms carry
# owner/admin/member/outcast affiliations keyed by bare JID, and MUCRoom in
# `xmppserver/src/main/java/org/jivesoftware/openfire/muc/MUCRoom.java`
# registered with the group lifecycle dispatcher (`GroupEventListener`) but
# never with the user one, so a user deletion reached only the database cleanup
# in `MultiUserChatManager.userDeleting` and no loaded room: in-memory
# affiliations survived, and the registration path matched a new account to
# them by bare JID.
#
# Fix `d66bddd29dbf56aa9b822635619fa66cca6f2112`
# ("OF-2795 / OF-2166: Remove MUC affiliation of a user that is being deleted.
# (#2420)") makes MUCRoom implement `UserEventListener`: `userDeleting()` takes
# the chat-room lock, keeps a room owned by replacing a sole owner with an
# administrative account, `addNone()`s the deleted user's JID and syncs the
# room. `LocalMUCRoomManager` registers and unregisters each room with
# `UserEventDispatcher`, and `MultiUserChatManager` keeps only the
# database-side removal.
#
# Held miss. The weakness is a missing revocation on an identity lifecycle
# event: no input flows anywhere, no operation runs on attacker-controlled
# data, and the vulnerable class is silent exactly where the fix adds a
# handler. Nothing in the vocabulary states "this per-identity privilege store
# must react to identity deletion", and the third block below is the reason no
# function-scope shape can be written instead: `MultiUserChatManager
# .userDeleting` is byte-for-byte the same body before and after the fix --
# one `removeAffiliationFromDB` call -- and it is correct after it, because
# what differs is which class holds the handler, not anything in the function.

test "rank1513: the room keeps affiliations when their user is deleted"
  lang java
  file xmppserver/src/main/java/org/jivesoftware/openfire/muc/MUCRoom.java
  reject VYQL-AUTH-079
  code
  ```
  package org.jivesoftware.openfire.muc;

  import java.util.Map;
  import java.util.concurrent.ConcurrentHashMap;
  import org.jivesoftware.openfire.event.GroupEventListener;
  import org.xmpp.packet.JID;

  public class MUCRoom implements GroupEventListener {
      private final Map<JID, MUCRole.Affiliation> affiliations = new ConcurrentHashMap<>();

      public MUCRole.Affiliation getAffiliation(JID userJid) {
          return affiliations.getOrDefault(userJid, MUCRole.Affiliation.none);
      }

      public void addNone(JID userJid) {
          affiliations.put(userJid, MUCRole.Affiliation.none);
      }

      public void groupCreated(String groupName, Map params) {}
      public void groupDeleting(String groupName, Map params) {}
      public void groupModified(String groupName, Map params) {}
  }
  ```

test "rank1513: the fixed revision revokes affiliations on user deletion"
  lang java
  file xmppserver/src/main/java/org/jivesoftware/openfire/muc/MUCRoom.java
  reject VYQL-AUTH-079
  code
  ```
  package org.jivesoftware.openfire.muc;

  import java.util.Map;
  import java.util.concurrent.locks.Lock;
  import org.jivesoftware.openfire.event.GroupEventListener;
  import org.jivesoftware.openfire.event.UserEventListener;
  import org.jivesoftware.openfire.user.User;

  public class MUCRoom implements GroupEventListener, UserEventListener {
      public void userDeleting(User user, Map<String, Object> params) {
          final JID userJid = XMPPServer.getInstance().createJID(user.getUsername(), null);
          final Lock lock = getMUCService().getChatRoomLock(getJID().getNode());
          try {
              lock.lock();
              if (getAffiliation(userJid) == MUCRole.Affiliation.none) {
                  return;
              }
              if (getOwners().contains(userJid) && getOwners().size() == 1) {
                  final JID adminJid = XMPPServer.getInstance().getAdmins().iterator().next();
                  addOwner(adminJid, getRole());
              }
              addNone(userJid, getRole());
              getMUCService().syncChatRoom(this);
          } finally {
              lock.unlock();
          }
      }

      public void userCreated(User user, Map<String, Object> params) {}
      public void userModified(User user, Map<String, Object> params) {}
      public void groupCreated(String groupName, Map params) {}
      public void groupDeleting(String groupName, Map params) {}
      public void groupModified(String groupName, Map params) {}
  }
  ```

test "rank1513: the manager's deletion handler body is the remediation-neutral form"
  lang java
  file xmppserver/src/main/java/org/jivesoftware/openfire/muc/MultiUserChatManager.java
  reject VYQL-AUTH-079
  code
  ```
  package org.jivesoftware.openfire.muc;

  import java.util.Map;
  import org.jivesoftware.openfire.XMPPServer;
  import org.jivesoftware.openfire.event.UserEventListener;
  import org.jivesoftware.openfire.user.User;

  public class MultiUserChatManager implements UserEventListener {
      public void userDeleting(User user, Map<String, Object> params) {
          MUCPersistenceManager
                  .removeAffiliationFromDB(XMPPServer.getInstance().createJID(user.getUsername(), null, true));
      }

      public void userCreated(User user, Map<String, Object> params) {}
      public void userModified(User user, Map<String, Object> params) {}
  }
  ```

# ## Recheck measurements
#
# Re-measured against the knowledge base as it stands, at the span tip.
# Three `--cache off` runs on the `org/jivesoftware/openfire/muc` package at
# `d66bddd29dbf^` and at `d66bddd29dbf`, `timeout 180` and `--max-ram 6GB`:
# **2 findings at both revisions, all six runs** -- `VYQL-AUTH-079`
# (`code.SensitiveOperation` by `auditreview.marks` on `role.setRole`) at
# MUCRoom.java:1803/1830 before and 1806/1833 after, the fix's three added
# import lines being the only difference. The whole tree gives **2888 findings
# at both revisions, all six runs, with an empty rule-count diff**, and the only
# rows on the three files the fix touches are those same two. Nothing
# later-shipped fires, and the earlier zero was not a misread: the full
# `-flags with` view was read too, 98 flags before and 100 after, where the
# +2 are the fix's own `code.LockAcquire`/`core.LockRelease` at
# MUCRoom.java:3838/3858 inside the new handler -- no auth flag touches the
# deletion path at either revision.
#
# The class-scope shape was probed and measured rather than asserted. Java does
# emit `class_base:` from the implements list (`java.go`, `walkTypes` over the
# `interfaces` field), so a scratch presence binding -- implements
# GroupEventListener, not UserEventListener -- is writable, and it discriminates
# **3 -> 2**: at `d66bddd29dbf^` it labels GroupEventAdapter.java:26,
# MUCRoom.java:63 and GroupTest.java:540, and at the fix only the adapter and
# the test remain. One true positive in three, and the two survivors are
# correct not to implement `UserEventListener`: `GroupEventAdapter` is the
# library's own no-op base class, and the third is a test. No documented anchor
# refines it -- `UserEventListener`'s javadoc says only "Interface to listen
# for group events. Use the addListener method to register" (a copy-paste from
# the group listener that never even says *user*), so nothing ties user-event
# listening to holding per-user state, and the pairing itself is ontology: 30
# classes under `src/main` implement some `*EventListener` across 18 interface
# kinds. A binding on Openfire's own listener interfaces detects this
# application's class layout, not the weakness.
#
# Function scope is impossible for a second, decisive reason:
# `MultiUserChatManager.userDeleting`, the only deletion handler that exists at
# the parent, is byte-identical minus comments across the fix (verified by
# diff), and correct after it -- the in-memory revocation lives in each room's
# own new handler. The nearest lifecycle vocabulary is still one step away on
# both edges: `code.LogoutWithoutSessionInvalidation` (`VYQL-AUTH-177`) has a
# PHP session emitter only, and `code.PublicVisibilityCacheInvalidationMissing`
# keys on visibility caches; no binding anywhere names `UserEventListener`,
# `UserEventDispatcher` or `GroupEventDispatcher` outside this spec.
#
# No definition is touched, so the OWASP Java and Python numbers are unaffected
# by this rank.
